ence, reflection and AI-use declaration.\n\n**Deadline:** 18 December 2026, 14:00 Kampala. The live form shows any later change.\n\n## 1. Start\n\nUse your completed project and repository. Open a blank PowerPoint presentation and a Word/Google Docs document.\n\n## 2. Prepare the evidence\n\n1. **Slides + narration notes (35 marks):** explain the engineering question, method, results and limitations.\n2. **Written defence (40):** answer the two prompts issued by the lecturer, citing your own cells/results.\n3. **Reflection (20):** write 500 words about progress since Week 1, remaining difficulties and two Semester 2 goals.\n4. **AI-use declaration (5):** describe assistance, or state none. Put items 2–4 in one document.\n\nAdd the repository URL and team ID where applicable. Live discussion is practice; submit the written evidence for grading.\n\n## 3. Hand in\n\nOpen your cohort’s form below. Enter your Student Registration Number. Choose **Add file → Upload**, attach the required file(s), then press **Submit**. Keep the confirmation.\n\nFor individual work, leave Team ID blank. Each file must be at most 10 MB. A replacement before the deadline must contain the complete file set again.\n\n**Do not submit:** the ZIP, guided_practice.ipynb, raw CSVs or helper scripts.\n\n**Submission link:** https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScnSPyvzCLfoHhIBusvFTIpqC-zAsk2bCLMqu6kJCh5cGI9gQ/viewform\n\n## Help only if needed\n\nNeed help opening, saving or uploading? Use STUDENT_HOW_TO.md only for the step you are stuck on. It is a reference, not extra homework.\n\nPreparation only until the lecturer releases this assessment. This guide does not open its form.\n"},{"key":"DA|S05","track":"DA","cohort":"DA","id":"S05","label":"S05","week":2,"title":"SQL analysis","maximum":100,"component":"DA","weight":15,"formats":["sql"],"opens_at":null,"closes_at":null,"preserve_existing":false,"grading":"sql","brief":"# DA S05 — SQL analysis\n\n## Start here\n\nUse your own DA learner ID from your roster. Sign in to upload with the Google account registered with the lecturer. If you need account or file help, read STUDENT_HOW_TO.md supplied with the student materials. A repository is a folder of saved project files; an upload form is where you hand in assessed work. Saving to a repository does not submit an assignment.\n\n## Do the work\n\n1. Install DB Browser for SQLite from https://sqlitebrowser.org/dl/ using the Windows or macOS installer for your computer, or use the prepared lab installation. It is a desktop application, not a website account; no database account is needed.\n2. Copy the supplied riverside.db into your session folder, open DB Browser, choose Open Database and select that copy. Choose Execute SQL. Follow the SQL lab examples before building the assessed query.\n3. Build the 2025 Delivered report, net of returns, with one row per rep/month. Return columns in this order: region, rep_name, month, gross_ugx, returned_ugx, net_ugx. Use rep region, retain walk-in orders and combine multiple return events before joining.\n4. Run your query using the Execute button. Check there are no duplicate rep/month keys, all months are in 2025, and net equals gross minus returns. Add a SQL comment identifying a rep to discuss and why; the discussion is formative.\n5. Save the SQL text from the Execute SQL editor as your learner ID followed by _S05.sql. If using a text editor, save plain text with a .sql extension, not .sql.txt. Reopen the file and execute it against the issued database.\n\n## Check and submit\n\nSubmit exactly ONE .sql file containing the final report query, optionally with comments beginning --. Do not submit the database, CSV result, screenshot or the entire lab exercise. Marks: row keys 25, gross 25, returns 25, net 25; amount tolerance is 1 UGX after rounding.\n\n## How to hand in\n\nOpen the correct cohort/assessment form below and read its current deadline. Fill the required identity fields; leave Team ID blank for individual work. Choose Add file > Upload and select the complete required file set. Wait for the filenames to appear, then click Submit. Keep the confirmation page and local copies. Each file must be at most 10 MB. If replacing work before the deadline, send the complete set again; the latest on-time response replaces the earlier response. If the form is closed or upload fails, keep your files and contact the lecturer; do not use a different assignment form.\n\nStudent submission link: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeFBg9-cA5rhj2h8fMBFy_XanpHAfvmpdZtPIgzMjSlZsBROA/viewform\n\nDates are not yet scheduled. Wait for the lecturer’s release and announced opening/deadline.\n\n"},{"key":"DA|S09","track":"DA","cohort":"DA","id":"S09","label":"S09","week":3,"title":"Data cleaning","maximum":100,"component":"DA","weight":15,"formats":["ipynb","pdf","docx"],"opens_at":null,"closes_at":null,"preserve_existing":false,"grading":"rubric","brief":"# DA S09 — Data cleaning\n\n## Start here\n\nUse your own DA learner ID from your roster. Sign in to upload with the Google account registered with the lecturer. If you need account or file help, read STUDENT_HOW_TO.md supplied with the student materials. A repository is a folder of saved project files; an upload form is where you hand in assessed work. Saving to a repository does not submit an assignment.\n\nOpen S09_student.ipynb as the assignment notebook. Keep these input/helper files in its runtime folder: customers.csv, customers_raw.csv, order_items.csv, order_items_raw.csv, orders.csv, orders_raw.csv, products.csv, reps.csv, returns.csv, riverside.db. In Colab use File > Open notebook > Upload for .ipynb; use the Files sidebar Upload button for data and .py helpers. Save your own notebook copy in Drive. Keep the issued raw files unchanged.\n\n## Do the work\n\n1. Read the task text above each answer cell before editing. Complete the supplied notebook rather than starting an unrelated notebook.\n2. Replace YOUR_ID in STUDENT_ID wi